WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.660
You may remember Who Framed Roger Rabbit as
a beloved animated film from your childhood,

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.780
but if you watch it as an adult, you might
be shocked at its explicit content.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.700
"So I took a couple'a dirty pictures, so kill
me."

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.280
"I already got a stiff on my hands, thank
you."

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.560
From the movie's colorful language to its
varied pop culture references, to the bodacious

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.040
Jessica Rabbit, here are some fascinating
things about Who Framed Roger Rabbit you only

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.120
notice as an adult.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:24.820
Pervy Herman

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:29.199
As kids we may have known something wasn't
right about a cartoon baby smoking a giant

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.020
cigar, but the sexual predilections of Baby
Herman may have gone over our innocent little

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:33.540
heads.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.120
"My problem is I got a 50-year-old's lust
and a three-year-old's dinky."

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.880
Baby Herman might look sweet, but that's just
a part he plays in the movies; Herman is a

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:42.880
total perv.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.920
When we first meet him, he takes a peek up
a crew member's skirt, and when Herman goes

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.680
to see Eddie, Herman slaps his lady friend
on the butt.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.500
Not exactly the kind of things you'd want
kids to emulate.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:53.720
Pattycake

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.440
When Eddie captures Jessica Rabbit and Marvin
Acme playing "pattycake" in her dressing room,

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.880
he thinks he's struck gold to convince Roger
that his wife is cheating on him.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.900
"Pattycake, pattycake, pattycake!"

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.340
Later, the pictures show that Jessica and
Marvin really were playing pattycake, but

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.500
judging by Roger's reaction, they may as well
have been playing bury the carrot instead.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.280
Whether or not pattycake is the toon equivalent
of sex, if you were under the age of 12 when

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.480
you saw this movie, hopefully the entire bit
would've been lost on you.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:24.260
Bad rabbit

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.360
As Jessica Rabbit says,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.400
"I'm not bad, I'm just drawn that way."

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.060
And in one case, that's definitely true.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.600
On the original Laserdisc version, when Jessica
and Eddie are in a high speed chase and thrown

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.640
from the taxi, there's a moment where her
dress reveals a little more than you'd expect

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:42.720
in a kids' movie.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.040
But of course, nothing about Jessica Rabbit
is exactly "family friendly."

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:47.780
"Nice booby trap."

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.000
Alcoholic Eddie

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.800
Eddie Valiant spends much of the movie in
Dolores' bar, and when he's not there, he's

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.000
back in his office, drowning his sorrows.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.980
Eddie's brother, who was also his partner,
was killed by a toon, and it's obvious that

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.940
Eddie is self-medicating his pain with the
help of booze.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.420
Watching the movie as an adult, it's obvious
that Eddie is a full-blown alcoholic.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:10.620
But the movie at least tries to warn its younger
audience members about the dangers of alcohol.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:14.900
When Roger is given a drink after discovering
Jessica's infidelity, the results are pretty

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:15.800
messy.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:17.640
Probate problems

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.859
In the scene where Eddie's trying to get his
handcuffs off, Eddie suggests that Dolores

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:27.120
go downtown to check the probate on Acme's
will, a word Roger clearly misinterprets.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:33.560
"My Uncle Thumper had a problem with his probate
and he had to take big pills and drink lots

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:34.080
of water!"

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:37.720
Chances are you had no idea what a prostate
was when you were a kid.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.280
Apparently neither does Roger.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:40.980
Inhuman Doom

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.719
The infamous shoe-melting scene in Who Framed
Roger Rabbit is one of the most traumatic

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.420
cinematic moments of our childhood.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:51.960
Thanks to Christopher Lloyd's terrifying performance,
Judge Doom goes down in the history books

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.240
as one of the scariest movie villains ever.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.460
It's hard to spot through his glasses—and
he has some animated help at the end of the

00:02:58.469 --> 00:03:01.930
movie—but hardcore fans of the film may
have noticed that Lloyd does something inhuman

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:04.800
in every scene he appears in: he never blinks.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:05.760
Terrifying.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.240
Invisible Harvey

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:11.519
When Judge Doom storms the bar looking for
Roger, one of the patrons seems like he's

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.170
about to give up Roger's hiding spot.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:14.580
And then he does this:

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:16.620
"Say hello, Harvey."

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.100
It's a pretty random joke… unless you get
the reference.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.090
The man at the bar was referring to Harvey,
a 1950 comedy about an invisible rabbit starring

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:30.780
Jimmy Stewart.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:35.080
And it just so happens that the main character
is an alcoholic like Eddie who spends most

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:35.920
of his time in a bar.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:37.540
Singing Sword

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:42.019
Looking for a weapon in the final showdown
with Judge Doom, Eddie grabs a prop out of

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.460
a box in the warehouse: the singing sword.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:51.980
"Oh, I know it's strictly taboooo!"

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.000
Older viewers probably recognized the sword
as none other than Frank Sinatra.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.120
But that's not the only pop culture reference
packed into this short bit at the end of the

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:00.620
film.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:05.060
Eddie's last name, Valiant, is a reference
to the Prince Valiant comic strip of the late

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:10.480
1930s and 40s—in it, Prince Valiant's weapon
of choice is… you guessed it, a singing

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:11.100
sword.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:12.800
Hand-drawn racism

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:17.090
Who Framed Roger Rabbit is packed with adult
themes, one of the biggest of which is its

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:18.740
depiction of racism in Hollywood.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.220
Cartoons are definitely being treated as second-class
citizens, especially under the reign of Judge

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.700
Doom, who is overtly racist against animated
figures.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.020
In the movie, the toons work as performers
and waiters at the club where Jessica sings,

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:32.680
but the only patrons are humans.

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:36.900
In the end, Roger Rabbit's achievements in
animation are only bolstered by this extended

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.860
metaphor on racism and segregation.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.760
It's fascinating to re-watch the movie as
an adult while keeping this metaphor in mind.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:48.040
"The best part is, they work for peanuts."

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.180
Thanks for watching!

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.480
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:53.980
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

